<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first performed 12 February <a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The song was written in 1797, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was gravely threatened by Revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. According to an account written in 1847 by Anton Schmid, custodian of the Austrian National Library, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-haydn">Joseph Haydn</a> had come to know &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and wished that Austria had a song like it to honour its sovereign.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The melody is Joseph Haydn's, written in 1797 for Lorenz Leopold Haschka's &quot;<b>Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser</b>&quot;, a birthday anthem for Emperor Francis II modelled on Britain's &quot;God Save the King&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser</b>&quot; was first performed on the birthday of Emperor Francis II, 12 February 1797, at the Burgtheater in Vienna, with the emperor present for his 29th birthday.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gott-erhalte-franz-den-kaiser">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser</a></li></ul></section>
